// THE SURGE — OVERLOAD — one song: what it plays, how it is arranged, how it sounds.
//
// THE SURGE as neurofunk drum & bass at 174 — the fastest song in the game for its
// fastest stage. A game alternate of surge, selectable from the dev menu (GAME
// ALTERNATES) and on the desk. Nothing replaces the live song.
// 
// The Surge riff is the spine: at full speed on a sync-razor lead with a plain square
// under it, an octave up in drop two, and at half speed (THEME: | Am | F | G | E |) on
// brass in the build, supersaw in the drops and a Reese bass line in the breakdown.
// The bass is the loop's own line on two song-local MRDR-3 growls — an FM-folded saw
// wobbling on the eighth, a synced saw with a sixteenth sample-and-hold filter — that
// switch every half bar in drop two, then stack. The Surge's kick (x.xx.xx.) is the
// IGNITION break verbatim, then its 3-3 accents live inside the two-step and on the
// orchestral brass stabs.
// 
//   1–4    IGNITION — the original loop at 174 on the D&B kit, getting bigger (cabinet loop)
//   5–12   build — half-time; THEME on brass; the Reese creeps in; snare roll, riser
//   13–28  DROP ONE — two-step, the growl on the Surge line, the riff screaming;
//          + THEME on supersaw from 21; half-time switch 25–26
//   29–44  CHANNEL SURF — eight 2-bar windows, each half-time with its cabinet's hook
//          on that cabinet's timbre over the Surge's bed, cut back to the riff by a
//          glitch (crush, tape-wobble, a one-beat hole, a buffer repeat, a tape-stop)
//   45–56  BREAKDOWN — Crypt's hook, then Frost's, then both over Dm6 Fmaj7 Dm6 E7;
//          the riff at half speed as a Reese bass line under them
//   57–64  build two — the riff through an opening filter, buffer-repeats, crush; the
//          chord lands on F, the Surge's own bVI, taken as V of B-flat minor
//   65–80  DROP TWO — a half step up: riff 8va, growl/screech switching then stacked,
//          Speed's riff (65–72) then Rhythm's Popcorn (73–80) as hockets on the square;
//          half-time switch 77–78; bar 80 tape-stops a semitone, so F becomes E, V of Am
//   81–96  EVERYTHING AT ONCE — A minor: riff + THEME + Cardboard + Neon (81–88), riff
//          8va + THEME + Field Service + Corporate Kombat (89–94); 95–96 glitch into bar 9
// 
// Where the quotes land (every hook at its own pitch — they are all in the Surge's key):
//   FIELD SERVICE      29–30 (square, pixel)         · 89–94 on the square
//   SPEED ZONE         31–32 (razor, tape wobble)    · 65–72 hocket on the square
//   TERMINAL VELOCITY  33–34 (alloy chime)           · 81–88 chime an octave up
//   FROST FORTRESS     35–36 (ice bell)              · 49–56 breakdown
//   CRYPT SHIFT        37–38 (theremin, tape-stop)   · 45–48, 53–56 breakdown
//   RHYTHM BANKRUPTCY  39–40 (square, crushed: LCD)  · 73–80 hocket on the square
//   CARDBOARD KINGDOM  41–42 (toy piano)             · 81–88 stacked under the riff
//   CORPORATE KOMBAT   43–44 (brass, power-down)     · 89–94 brass counter-line
// 
// Loops: the cabinet screen loops bars 1–4; the song loops 9–96 (88 bars,
// 121.4 s), re-entering the build four bars before drop one.
// 
// 96 bars at 174, 132 s. Written by work/local/_surge-remix-overload.mjs through
// work/local/_remix-lib.mjs and the shared quotes in work/local/_surge-quotes.mjs;
// re-running it rewrites this file.
//
// SPOT FX (1 Oct 2026, work/local/_spot-fx-surge.mjs — re-run it after a regeneration): each
// channel-surf seam is a real device on the master — 30 repeat + crush, 32 the razor wobbling
// (tremolo + crush, on time), 34 a true hole, 36 a repeat, 38 a tape stop (lowpass closing),
// 39–40 the whole mix crushed (the LCD), 42 a repeat, 44 a power-down into the breakdown. Build
// two stutters at 61 and 63 and rolls into a hole at 64; 80 and 96 power down. No TAPE snapshots.
//
// The music below is the composition. Everything under THE DESK WRITES BELOW HERE
// is written by `npm run mixer` and will be rewritten on every save — put notes
// about the song up here, where they survive.
